// Reviewed against the shipped controls. This is trusted editorial HTML, never sample data.
export const guideEdition='SPECTRA 0.12.1 · Field guide, October 2026';
export const chapters=[
{id:'start',title:'Start here',view:null,summary:'Your first investigation, the shared controls, and how to keep a useful trail.',body:`
<h3>One question at a time</h3><p>Reverse engineering means working backward from an artifact to understand how it is put together and what it might do. The artifact could be a desktop client, a firmware image, a library, or a honeypot download. You do not need to identify the whole program at once. Pick a question you can check: “Where is this hostname used?” or “What changes between these two builds?”</p>
<p>SPECTRA puts several views of the same bytes in one browser workspace. Strings give you leads. Structure tells you how the file is arranged. References connect data to code. Disassembly and decompilation help explain the logic. The debugger lets you test a small piece with explicit inputs. Notebook keeps the reasoning that connects those steps.</p>
<h3>A first pass you can actually finish</h3><ol><li>Save any open investigation, then use <strong>Open file</strong> or drop a file into the workbench. The general file limit is 64 MiB; individual engines have smaller limits.</li><li>Record the filename and <strong>SHA-256</strong>. The hash identifies these exact bytes, even if someone renames the file.</li><li>Visit <strong>File structure</strong>. Read the format, CPU, entry point, sections, and imports. For an unidentified raw blob, start with Strings and Hex.</li><li>Use <strong>Detect</strong> when a compiler, runtime, packer, or protector fingerprint would help you choose a path.</li><li>Pick one interesting string. Star it, write why it matters, then choose <strong>Find code references</strong>.</li><li>Read the caller in Disassembly, and reconstruct the function in Decompile if supported. Check what uses the value, not just whether it exists.</li><li>Write a conclusion and the evidence still missing in Notebook. Export an investigation project before you stop.</li></ol>
<h3>The controls shared by every section</h3><dl><dt>Current sample card</dt><dd>Shows the active filename, size, detected format/architecture, hash, and analysis status. A demo badge means the loaded bytes are training material. Loading a demo or another sample replaces the active investigation.</dd><dt>Analysis overview</dt><dd>Extracted strings are readable candidates; indicator counts are pattern matches; file entropy is a byte-distribution measurement; section/import counts describe the parsed file. None is a risk score.</dd><dt>Byte landscape</dt><dd>Each grayscale bar summarizes entropy in a file region. Click one to inspect its file offset in Hex. Bright regions can contain compression, encryption, or other dense data.</dd><dt>Explain as I go</dt><dd>Turns the optional contextual teaching panel on or off. It changes the help you see, not the analysis or features available.</dd><dt>Export findings</dt><dd>JSON, CSV, and text export the current string selection/filter with different amounts of context. Choose an investigation project to preserve the broader workspace.</dd></dl>
<h3>Addresses: get this straight early</h3><p>A <strong>file offset</strong> is a position in the file on disk. A <strong>virtual address (VA)</strong> is an address in the program’s mapped memory. An <strong>RVA</strong> is relative to an image base. They may look similar and still refer to different places. Hex and raw file ranges use offsets. Code navigation and debugger memory use VAs. Enter decimal numbers or prefix hexadecimal with <code>0x</code>.</p><p>For a file-backed mapping, if file offset <code>0x400</code> maps to VA <code>0x401000</code>, then offset <code>0x420</code> maps to VA <code>0x401020</code>. That relationship only holds inside that mapping. Zero-filled memory can have no bytes in the file at all.</p>
<h3>Keep the claim as small as the evidence</h3><p>“These bytes contain a URL” is an observation. “This function passes that URL to a network API” is a stronger static finding. “The program contacted it during a real run” needs runtime evidence. SPECTRA’s emulated traces describe its model and your supplied state. Keeping these distinctions makes your notes useful to the next researcher.</p>
<h3>Quick keys and common questions</h3><dl><dt>Phone or tablet</dt><dd>Use Open a tool to switch sections, Open file for the device picker, and the experiment forms and debugger buttons for touch input. The tools have no paid mobile tier.</dd><dt>Keyboard shortcuts</dt><dd><code>O</code> opens a file, <code>/</code> focuses string search, <code>B</code> toggles the selected string’s bookmark, <code>H</code> opens its bytes, and <code>?</code> opens the shortcut list. Shortcuts do not fire while typing in a form field.</dd><dt>Does opening a sample run it?</dt><dd>Opening parses bytes locally. Debugger execution requires explicit controls. The DiE scanner runs locally to inspect the sample without launching the sample. A generated harness is a separate download and can execute target code when you deliberately use it outside SPECTRA.</dd><dt>Does the Church get my binary?</dt><dd>The analysis app does not upload sample bytes. Your browser requests the page and engine assets from the Church; the delivery service sees ordinary request metadata. Project exports, copied notes, and downloaded bytes are yours to store or share deliberately.</dd><dt>Where should I begin if I know nothing yet?</dt><dd>Read the Strings chapter, then try Training ground’s ELF exercise. Follow one reference all the way to a note. That is a complete, useful first investigation.</dd></dl>`},
{id:'strings',title:'Strings',view:'strings',summary:'Readable bytes, indicator filters, the inspector, bookmarks, and useful leads.',body:`
<h3>What this section is for</h3><p>Strings finds readable runs of bytes without executing the file. Error messages, paths, URLs, class names, commands, and configuration fragments often tell you where to start. Ordinary software contains the same kinds of text as malware. Treat a string as a lead with an address, not a conclusion.</p>
<h3>Read the table and inspector</h3><p>Each row has a bookmark star, a <strong>file offset</strong>, a text preview, and a tag or encoding hint. Select the row to see its encoding, character/byte lengths, section, surrounding bytes, and analyst note. The highlighted byte preview shows which bytes belong to the selection.</p><p>SPECTRA extracts printable ASCII and ASCII-range wide strings. UTF-16 LE/BE refers to byte order for the wide representation; this is not a full multilingual Unicode text recovery engine. The default combines ASCII and UTF-16LE. <strong>All encodings</strong> includes UTF-16BE candidates too.</p>
<h3>Work through the controls</h3><ol><li>Search a word or a hexadecimal file offset. Search is case-insensitive text matching, not a regular-expression engine.</li><li>Choose an encoding and minimum length. Four characters is the minimum; increasing it cuts noise but can hide short useful values.</li><li>Use <strong>Indicators</strong>, URLs, IPs, Paths, Commands, APIs, or Saved to narrow the table. These are heuristic categories, not validated destinations or behavior.</li><li>Use <strong>Unique values</strong> for a less repetitive overview. Turn it off before studying every occurrence: the same text at different offsets can have different users.</li><li>Select a row. Star it and add a note such as “possible update endpoint; caller not checked yet.”</li><li>Choose <strong>View in hex</strong>, <strong>Find code references</strong>, or <strong>Decode</strong> according to the question.</li></ol>
<h3>Follow a useful example</h3><p>In Training ground, load the ELF exercise. Find <code>SPECTRA_FIXTURE_ONLY.example</code>. Record its offset, then ask for code references. Follow the referring instruction into its function. The goal is to explain how the program uses the marker. No lookup or connection to the hostname is needed.</p>
<h3>Exports and missing text</h3><p>CSV and plain-text exports follow your current filters. JSON carries more context, including metadata and notes. Long row previews can be clipped at 4,096 characters; use the byte lengths, full-text copy/export, and Hex when a complete value matters. Results can also hit the overall extraction cap; read the warning banner before describing the list as exhaustive.</p>
<h3>FAQ / when the obvious lead goes nowhere</h3><dl><dt>Why are there no interesting strings?</dt><dd>They may be compressed, encrypted, assembled at runtime, stored in an unsupported encoding, or absent from this file. Check Detect and structure before trying Recovery lab.</dd><dt>Why does a real hostname have no references?</dt><dd>Code may calculate its address, use a table, or reference a containing buffer. The string may also be unused. Static references are incomplete.</dd><dt>Is a Base64 tag a decoded secret?</dt><dd>No. It marks text shaped like Base64. Send it to Decoder lab and inspect the result and original context.</dd><dt>Why did a saved note reappear?</dt><dd>Lightweight notes/bookmarks are stored on this browser by sample hash. Full investigations need the Notebook save/export controls.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Use Cross-references to find users of the text, Hex to verify bytes, Decoder for one copied value, or Recovery for file-backed layers. Save the string and your reasoning in Notebook before changing samples.</p>`},
{id:'code',title:'Disassembly',view:'code',summary:'Instructions, raw mappings, blocks, and the context around a selected address.',body:`
<h3>Indirect calls and switch tables</h3><p>A call through a register can hide its destination. SPECTRA now follows supported x86/x64 and AArch64 address-building sequences inside one basic block. The reference card keeps the instructions behind the result. Calls, joins and unsupported operations drop those facts. A file pointer is still a candidate: runtime code may change it. Writable slots do not automatically create new code paths.</p><p>Open <strong>Indirect targets &amp; switch tables</strong>. Supported unsigned range checks can reveal x86 absolute or x64 relative switch tables. Expand a table to read each case label, stored value, entry file offset and target. Case edges also appear in Blocks and Cross-references. Unsupported patterns stay unresolved.</p><ol><li>Select the dispatch instruction and choose <strong>Use selected instruction</strong>.</li><li>For one target, enter its VA and why you believe the relationship, then Save indirect target.</li><li>For a table, enter its VA, count, entry width, absolute/signed-relative format, relative base and first case label. Save table hypothesis.</li><li>Check every resulting entry against Hex and the code. Remove the hypothesis to recompute without it.</li></ol><p>Hypotheses change static discovery; they do not patch bytes, hook the debugger or override Ghidra control flow. Tables are limited to 256 entries; all must fit mapped bytes and target aligned code. The form does not guess scaled offsets or processor-specific PC biases. Try <strong>Load analysis demo</strong> and inspect dispatch for a small three-way example.</p>
<h3>What you are looking at</h3><p>Disassembly translates machine-code bytes into instructions for a selected CPU. SPECTRA uses Capstone. The table shows <strong>VA</strong>, <strong>file offset</strong>, <strong>bytes</strong>, and the instruction’s mnemonic/operands. A plausible instruction list only means those bytes decode under those settings; it does not prove the whole region is executable code.</p>
<h3>A normal PE or ELF workflow</h3><ol><li>Open the binary and choose <strong>Analyze code</strong>. Read any mapping, truncation, or unsupported-architecture warning.</li><li>Choose a recovered function in the Function selector, or leave all decoded instructions visible.</li><li>Filter by an instruction, operand, or address. <strong>Jump to VA</strong> expects a memory address, not a file offset.</li><li>Select an instruction. Read its outgoing references and references to that address in <strong>Instruction context</strong>.</li><li>Follow a call or data reference. Use <strong>View bytes</strong> to check the underlying file location, <strong>Decompile function</strong> for reconstructed logic, or <strong>Name / annotate</strong> to record your interpretation.</li></ol>
<h3>Keep your place and compare the views</h3><p><strong>Back</strong> and <strong>Forward</strong> follow the local investigation navigation history. Jump through a reference, inspect it, and return to the earlier code location. The history is bounded to 128 positions and resets when a different sample opens.</p><p>Turn on <strong>Show reconstructed C beside assembly</strong>, select a function, and choose <strong>Reconstruct selected function</strong>. On a wide screen the views sit together; on a phone the C pane follows the assembly. SPECTRA only pairs a native result whose sample hash and function entry match. This is a shared function view, not an instruction-to-source line map. Use <strong>Edit signature / types</strong> to supply actual Ghidra assumptions, then reconstruct again.</p><p><strong>Blocks</strong> includes a scrollable graph and the detailed block list. Solid edges show resolved next/taken paths; dashed edges show fallthrough. Keyboard users can focus a graph block and press Enter to open it. The drawing is capped at 64 blocks; the list retains the existing 250-block display cap. Indirect or unresolved edges are not invented.</p>
<h3>Raw range / architecture override</h3><p>Use this when analyzing a headerless firmware fragment, a known code range, or a file whose metadata is insufficient. Set <strong>File offset</strong> and <strong>Byte length</strong> to choose the bytes; set <strong>Base VA</strong> to the address of the first selected byte; then select CPU and byte order and choose <strong>Decode range</strong>.</p><p>For example, a 64-byte code fragment at file offset <code>0x200</code> that loads at <code>0x80001000</code> uses those two different numbers in Offset and Base VA. Guessing the base can leave absolute references wrong even when the instructions look sensible. Choose Thumb for Thumb instructions; ARM32 and Thumb are not interchangeable settings. Big/little endian also changes interpretation.</p>
<h3>Assembly, blocks, and instruction context</h3><dl><dt>Assembly</dt><dd>The instruction list, with pagination and selected-function filtering. A highlighted row is your current location.</dd><dt>Blocks</dt><dd>Groups reachable instructions into basic blocks and shows their control-flow relationships. A basic block usually runs straight through until a branch, return, or another boundary. Read the conditions and successors to understand paths.</dd><dt>Outgoing references</dt><dd>Resolved targets leaving the instruction: code, data, string locations, or import slots. An indirect call’s slot can be named even when the runtime target remains unknown.</dd><dt>References to this address</dt><dd>Known decoded instructions that lead here. Missing incoming references do not mean the location is unreachable.</dd><dt>Function start / call relationships</dt><dd>Shows why a start was selected and its reachable instruction count. Follow call relationships to see known callers/callees.</dd></dl>
<h3>Reading a tiny routine</h3><p>A load from a pointer followed by an addition and a return gives you a hypothesis: “read input, adjust it, return the result.” Check the load width, signedness, pointer origin, and calling convention before writing a C-like explanation. Branches may skip that path. The debugger can check one carefully supplied case.</p>
<h3>FAQ / troubleshooting</h3><dl><dt>The output looks like nonsense.</dt><dd>Check architecture, bit width, byte order, alignment, code-versus-data, and packing. Change only a setting you have evidence for.</dd><dt>Why is some code missing?</dt><dd>The pass is bounded: up to 60,000 instructions and 4 MiB of decoded bytes, with a worker deadline. Indirect flow and uncertain entry points also limit coverage. Try a smaller justified raw range.</dd><dt>Does Debug from here immediately run the instruction?</dt><dd>It prepares the debugger location. Review the environment and initialization before execution.</dd><dt>Can I save the result?</dt><dd><strong>Export code JSON</strong> keeps computed code evidence. Use a Notebook project for annotations and the broader investigation.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Functions organizes entry points; Cross-references follows links; Decompile gives a higher-level view; Debugger lab tests a bounded routine. Keep addresses attached to every claim.</p>`},
{id:'decompile',title:'Decompile',view:'decompile',summary:'Native C, managed C#, types, dependencies, clues, and downloadable .NET harnesses.',body:`
<h3>Put names on the fields</h3><p>Open <strong>Structure workspace</strong> after choosing the intended native architecture and ABI. Give the layout a name and total byte size. Add ordered fields with offsets, fixed-width types, names and counts. Save structure keeps it with the investigation; the generated declaration shows any padding. Misaligned, overlapping or oversized fields are rejected. This form supports ordinary integer layouts, not automatic C++ class recovery.</p><p>For example, save Record with uint32_t tag at offset 0 and uint32_t count at offset 4. In the function signature use <code>uint32_t read_count(Record *record);</code>, check Apply and save this function’s assumptions, then Decompile. Ghidra can now print <code>record-&gt;count</code>. Its prototype parser uses <code>Record *</code> after registering the structure, rather than <code>struct Record *</code>.</p><p>Read fields from file previews the supplied layout at a <strong>file offset</strong>, with the selected byte order. It does not follow pointers or prove that the structure is correct. Arrays show up to 16 values. Limits: 32 structures, 64 fields each, 64 KiB per layout. Packed layouts, bitfields and nested types require supported manual declarations.</p><h3>Make corrections travel</h3><p>After successful reconstruction, a saved signature informs other functions—including callers—using the same full Ghidra language and ABI. Shared structure declarations travel with it. A changed name or type marks previous native C as out of date; re-decompile instead of trusting stale output. The paired pane also requires a fresh result. Rejected signatures do not overwrite saved assumptions. Other architectures and the second binary in a comparison keep their own context.</p>
<h3>Reconstructed source is a working explanation</h3><p>A decompiler lifts lower-level code into a more readable form. Native code uses the Ghidra engine; managed .NET uses ILSpy. The output is reconstructed logic, not the original developer’s source tree. Optimizations can erase variable names, merge operations, inline functions, and rearrange control flow. Cross-check important conditions and memory accesses against assembly or IL evidence.</p>
<h3>Native code: reconstruct one function</h3><ol><li>Choose <strong>Ghidra · native / firmware</strong>. Start from a known function in Disassembly or choose <strong>Find functions</strong>.</li><li>Select a function, use <strong>Use selected function</strong>, or enter its <strong>Function VA</strong>. Choose a real function entry rather than the middle of a random instruction stream.</li><li>Check architecture, byte order, and calling convention. “From file” relies on parsed metadata; an override is your explicit assumption.</li><li>Choose <strong>Decompile</strong>. Read the engine’s warnings along with the code.</li><li>Compare the result with the caller, imports, and assembly. <strong>Copy code</strong> or <strong>Export source</strong> keeps the text; <strong>Export evidence</strong> keeps the reconstruction’s context.</li></ol>
<p><strong>Native demo</strong> and <strong>.NET demo</strong> load benign examples for the two engines. <strong>Load benign hardware demo</strong> prepares a small native example for a hardware profile. These replace the active sample, so save first. The expandable <strong>Native imports</strong> table is another place to check static import names when the reconstructed code makes an indirect call.</p>
<h3>Raw firmware and ABI settings</h3><p>Under <strong>Raw firmware mapping &amp; ABI assumptions</strong>, select a raw range and provide file offset, byte length, and load VA. Architecture and endianness must describe that range. SPECTRA supports native reconstruction paths for x86/x64, ARM/Thumb, AArch64, RISC-V 32/64 with compressed instructions, and MIPS32/64; support is not a promise to understand every board or vendor extension.</p><p>The <strong>calling convention / ABI</strong> controls where arguments, results, and preserved registers belong. A wrong convention can make a good instruction decode turn into bad pseudocode. RISC-V metadata mismatches may require the explicit double-float ABI acceptance control. Use it only when you mean that assumption; it does not add floating-point hardware emulation. Raw mapping likewise does not recover missing firmware memory or peripherals.</p>
<h3>Tell Ghidra the types you know</h3><p>If you have evidence for a function signature or a type declaration, enable <strong>Apply and save this function’s assumptions</strong>, enter the signature and supported declaration, and reconstruct again. The parser must accept the declaration. Start small and check warnings. A correct pointer or structure type can make accesses much clearer; an invented type can make wrong output look convincing.</p><p>Notebook’s <strong>Function signature hypothesis</strong> and <strong>Variable / type notes</strong> are annotations. They do not automatically change engine types. Use this Decompile panel when you intend to feed assumptions to Ghidra, and keep the evidence for them in your notes.</p>
<h3>.NET: types, methods, and local dependencies</h3><ol><li>Choose <strong>ILSpy · .NET / C#</strong> and <strong>Browse .NET types</strong>. The main assembly limit is 16 MiB.</li><li>Search the <strong>Types &amp; methods</strong> tree by type or method signature. Expand a type; click a method to reconstruct it, or choose <strong>Decompile type</strong>.</li><li>Use <strong>Add local .NET dependencies</strong> for assemblies you already have. SPECTRA resolves exact assembly identity rather than fetching missing files from the internet. Up to 32 dependencies, 16 MiB each and 64 MiB total, are admitted.</li><li>Read missing-reference warnings. A matching filename alone does not guarantee matching name, version, culture, and public-key identity. <strong>Clear dependencies</strong> removes the supplied set.</li><li>Use category filters and <strong>Clues</strong> to examine crypto, auth, network, serialization, and filesystem evidence.</li></ol>
<h3>What the method labels mean</h3><p>A label tells you why a method is worth a look. Its evidence table records the category, basis, token/IL location, and triggering call, reference, name, or string. An IL call is stronger than a suggestive method name; declared API names can still be spoofed. A crypto label does not establish a weakness, and an auth label does not prove the server trusts the client. Follow the data and check which side enforces the decision.</p><p>Generated async/iterator state-machine methods may contain the interesting instructions; the clue display calls out that relationship. The tree is bounded to keep the page usable. Narrow the search if a large assembly clips the visible list.</p>
<h3>.NET Harness lab: turn a finding into a repeatable experiment</h3><p>A harness is a small driver that loads a target assembly and calls selected methods with controlled setup and inputs. It is useful when studying an owned desktop client, a serializer, or a protocol helper. SPECTRA generates the harness; the browser does not execute it or automatically reproduce a working client/server session.</p><ol><li>Tick methods, choose <strong>Add class to harness</strong>, or select <strong>Add entire assembly</strong> for a small target. Prefer a focused set; the selection cap is 2,000 methods.</li><li>Choose the <strong>First method</strong>, runner SDK (.NET 8, .NET 10, or the experimental .NET Framework 4.8 host), and target CPU. Match the target’s real runtime and native dependencies.</li><li>Open <strong>Reusable setup recipe &amp; target CPU</strong> if you need saved setup. The optional invocation recipe is JSON; follow the generated runner’s schema/examples rather than pasting arbitrary prose.</li><li><strong>Export selection + clues</strong> to preserve the research evidence, or <strong>Download harness ZIP</strong> for the runnable project.</li><li>Read its README before using it in your own isolated, authorized lab. The modern runner’s default preview does not load the client; explicit execution can. Supply the actual matching assemblies and environment yourself.</li></ol><p>Instance construction, UI threads, login/session state, complex objects, COM, native libraries, and protocol setup may need edits in the generated hooks. Proxy hooks help where the target uses compatible networking; they are not universal interception. Certificate pinning, custom transports, and server requirements are not automatically solved. Framework 4.8 projects are compile-tested; Windows execution is not validated.</p>
<h3>FAQ / dead ends</h3><dl><dt>Why do I see strange names or casts?</dt><dd>Names/types may be missing or inferred. Rename only what you understand and inspect the underlying operation before simplifying a cast away.</dd><dt>The native decompiler fails or times out.</dt><dd>Check the function entry and mapping, ABI, supported processor, input size (32 MiB), and whether the bytes are packed/data. Try one smaller, justified function. Cancellation leaves an incomplete result, not proof the file is invalid.</dd><dt>ILSpy could not load its engine. Is my sample broken?</dt><dd>That message means the engine failed before it inspected your file. Read the error detail, then use <strong>Retry ILSpy</strong>; your sample and local dependencies stay open. If it repeats, check your connection or site access. For an offline release, use its included local server and keep the engine folders. Once metadata loads, missing-reference warnings are a separate issue: supply matching local assemblies if you have them, or review the partial reconstruction.</dd><dt>Is every Windows executable .NET?</dt><dd>No. PE is a container format used by native and managed code. A native PE belongs in the Ghidra path; a supported managed assembly belongs in ILSpy.</dd><dt>Can the harness contact a live service?</dt><dd>Only when you run target code with the necessary environment outside the browser. Use a service you own or have permission to test and a controlled test account. Generation alone does not contact it.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Check decisive code in Disassembly, test a small supported native routine in Debugger lab, compare a patch in Binary diff, or save reconstructed source, assumptions, dependencies, and harness choices in Notebook.</p>`},
{id:'debugger',title:'Debugger lab',view:'debugger',summary:'Function harnesses, recipes, Windows/Linux models, ARM MMIO, coverage, batches, watches and replay.',body:`
<h3>Build inputs without hand-editing JSON</h3><p>Open <strong>Build inputs with forms</strong> under Experiment setup. Choose arguments, buffers, virtual files, supplied API/function responses, ARM hardware-register responses, captured memory or CPU registers. Fill one entry, then Add. Existing entries have Edit and Remove controls. The forms and advanced JSON share the same validation and update the same recipe.</p><p>For an integer, type decimal or <code>0x</code> hexadecimal. For a pointer, name a buffer and give its offset. Buffers and virtual files accept UTF-8 text or exact hex. Hardware reads accept a sequence of values; choose whether exhaustion stops or holds the final value. These are your supplied responses, not observed hardware behavior. Changes take effect on <strong>Initialize session</strong>; editing a form does not run an instruction.</p>
<h3>Start with the right environment</h3><p>Opening a sample selects its CPU, byte order and PE/ELF process model. <strong>Use detected setup</strong> restores those defaults after manual changes. The setup check runs the same loader validation used by initialization, without running instructions. <strong>Loader checks passed</strong> means the initial image can be mapped; it is not a promise the whole program can run.</p><ol><li>Read the detected CPU and format. ARM code needs ARM, not an x86 shell. Raw bytes need you to supply a CPU, range and address.</li><li>Read <strong>Missing / incompatible</strong> before starting. Linux interpreter paths, declared shared libraries, PIE, TLS and unsupported CPU profiles are shown here. Adding a library as a virtual file does not implement a dynamic linker.</li><li>For Windows, expand <strong>Windows import coverage</strong>. Each imported slot is modeled, missing, or covered by an analyst response. Missing calls stop if reached. Runtime-resolved calls can add requirements that static imports cannot predict.</li><li>Set up your inputs and choose <strong>Initialize session</strong>. Nothing has executed. Check Session memory map, stack and registers, then set a breakpoint and Step.</li><li>If full entry-point startup needs unsupported runtime behavior, select a useful function in Disassembly and choose <strong>Use selected function</strong>. That explicitly switches to Bare routine. Supply its calling convention, arguments and dependencies rather than treating it as a whole process.</li></ol><p>If you change setup while a session is active, a notice separates the proposed setup from the running session. Initialize replaces it with the new setup; Reset repeats the original session configuration. Save a checkpoint first if you need the current experiment.</p>
<h3>Linux process inputs and observations</h3><p>The Linux profile maps static ET_EXEC segments, preserves permissions, zeroes BSS, and builds a pointer-width-correct argc/argv/env/auxv stack. It supports little-endian x86, x64, ARM/Thumb EABI5 and AArch64. Thumb entry state is selected from the ELF entry bit. argv, environment variables and stdin are recipe inputs. Startup random bytes are deliberately deterministic and labeled as model state.</p><p>The bounded syscall set includes read/write, vector I/O, open/openat, close, seek, anonymous mappings, protection changes, brk, exit and selected process identity/path queries. Calls use virtual bytes only. uname describes a synthetic system; it is not evidence of a booted kernel. Unknown calls stop before a fabricated result is supplied. Dynamic linkers, ELF TLS, PIE, threads, signals, networking and general libc compatibility are not provided.</p><p>Choose <strong>Add local input file</strong> with the virtual path your code expects to attach a data file. Each file is at most 64 KiB; recipe inputs total 128 KiB. Review and remove entries in the input builder. This does not load native DLL/shared-library code. Linux paths are absolute. Initialize to apply input changes. Output files can go to Recovery lab with the session’s model assumptions and trace.</p><p>Session memory map is available for every CPU and environment. Click a mapped address to inspect its live bytes. Syscall writes appear in the execution trace and trigger memory watchpoints. Recipe/Linux events include names, arguments and results. Checkpoint replay verifies the modeled process state alongside registers and memory.</p>
<h3>What this debugger does</h3><p>The browser debugger uses Unicorn CPU emulation. It gives a routine mapped memory, registers, and a bounded environment, then lets you observe instructions. It is useful for checking an argument, following a branch, or catching a decoded string. This workspace models routines and selected process behavior. The setup check reads the file’s CPU and format before you initialize; it shows missing environment support instead of sending you to a separate shell.</p><p><strong>Initialize session</strong> sets up the experiment without executing a sample instruction. <strong>Step</strong> or <strong>Run / continue</strong> starts emulated execution. The original file stays unchanged. Results depend on the mapped bytes, model, and every input you supply.</p>
<h3>Set up a routine</h3><ol><li>Choose <strong>Bare routine</strong> for a small function, or <strong>Windows process model</strong> for the supported PE environment.</li><li>Select CPU and byte order. Set <strong>Start VA</strong>, or use the function selected in code analysis.</li><li>For raw bytes, open <strong>Load base, raw bytes &amp; session assumptions</strong>. Supply file offset, length, and raw base VA. A Windows load-base override is a different setting used for PE rebasing.</li><li>Initialize. Read the status, register values, memory map, and assumptions. An initialized pointer is not necessarily a meaningful input for your function.</li><li>Set arguments according to the actual calling convention. Put pointed-to data in mapped scratch memory with a session patch, then put that VA in the argument register. Zero does not stand in for a real object.</li><li>Save a baseline checkpoint, then step a few instructions before doing a longer run.</li></ol>
<h3>Execution controls</h3><dl><dt>Step instruction</dt><dd>Advances one supported step. MIPS branch/delay-slot pairs are handled together; “step” is not always one displayed row on that architecture.</dd><dt>Step over</dt><dd>Runs through a call toward the following instruction within the budget. It can stop earlier on a breakpoint, watchpoint, unsupported operation, or fault.</dd><dt>Step out</dt><dd>Follows observed calls toward a return. It is not a general unwinder for arbitrary stack manipulation or execution started halfway through a call chain.</dd><dt>Run / continue and Run budget</dt><dd>Execute bounded work until a stop condition. A budget stop is not the program’s natural exit. Raise work in small increments only after you understand the current state.</dd><dt>Pause</dt><dd>Requests a pause between execution batches. A worker deadline can terminate a stuck job; the last displayed snapshot may then be incomplete.</dd><dt>Reset session / End session</dt><dd>Reset rebuilds the initial setup. End discards the active emulator; when idle, it attempts to retain a checkpoint for workspace saving. Save a checkpoint explicitly before ending anything important.</dd></dl>
<h3>Breakpoints and conditional breaks</h3><p>Enter a <strong>Breakpoint VA</strong> and choose <strong>Add breakpoint</strong> to stop before reaching an address. Under <strong>Conditional breakpoints</strong>, pick a register, comparison, and unsigned value, then add the condition at that breakpoint VA. These are register comparisons, not arbitrary expressions or script evaluation.</p><p>Continue skips the current breakpoint once so it can make progress. On MIPS, a delay-slot breakpoint pauses before the branch/slot pair and sees the pre-pair registers. If a condition never hits, check both the address and whether execution actually reaches it. Remove controls using their chips when they no longer answer your question.</p>
<h3>Registers and session memory</h3><p>Registers hold values and addresses currently available to the CPU. Changed values are highlighted. Flags describe recent instruction effects used by conditional branches. <strong>Set a register</strong> records your edit; do not confuse an analyst-supplied value with a recovered runtime fact.</p><p><strong>Read memory</strong> reads a memory VA. <strong>Show scratch</strong> and <strong>Show stack</strong> jump to provided working areas. <strong>Patch session bytes</strong> writes up to 4 KiB of supplied hex into mapped emulator memory. This changes the experiment’s copy, not the file in Hex explorer.</p><p>Set <strong>Capture bytes</strong> and choose <strong>Send memory to Recovery lab</strong> to preserve a decoded buffer with its address, sample hash, steps, and assumptions. A correct-looking string under your model is valuable evidence; it is not proof that every real run produces it.</p>
<h3>Watch memory change</h3><p>Under <strong>Watch memory</strong>, choose the start VA, size, and read/write access to watch. A watchpoint pauses after the instruction or modeled API access, so the write may already be visible. Start with a short output buffer instead of watching a whole image. Inspect the hit and the preceding instruction, then capture the relevant bytes.</p>
<h3>Save, replay, and compare experiments</h3><ol><li>Pause and name a baseline in <strong>Saved experiments</strong>. Choose <strong>Save checkpoint</strong>; up to eight are retained.</li><li>Change one input, step/run the same routine, and save a second checkpoint.</li><li>Select From and To and choose <strong>Compare &amp; export</strong>. Compare registers, mapped-page digests/permissions, and applicable Windows/Linux/recipe state, events and files. Differences establish changed state in these experiments, not universal behavior.</li><li>Use <strong>Download</strong> to keep an experiment or <strong>Import checkpoint</strong> to bring one back. It needs the same sample and engine build.</li><li><strong>Restore by replay</strong> reruns recorded attempts and edits in a fresh emulator and verifies the resulting state before adopting it. It does execute emulated instructions; importing evidence by itself does not.</li></ol><p>A full Notebook workspace can preserve the current experiment too. After reopening it, choose <strong>Restore workspace’s current emulator state</strong> when you want to perform the replay. A failed verification is a failed restore, not a checkpoint you should assume worked.</p>
<h3>Windows environment &amp; API trace</h3><p>The supported x86/x64 PE model provides mapping/rebasing, imports, TLS handling, and a bounded set of 56 modeled APIs. Inspect <strong>Memory map</strong>, API arguments/results, model notes, debug output, and virtual files. The newest 500 API events are retained. Clicking a virtual file sends its captured bytes to Recovery; those paths do not create files on your real filesystem.</p><p>Unknown APIs and unsupported operations stop. Guest networking is not supplied. There is no complete Windows kernel, GUI environment, or CLR managed debugger here. “It stopped at an API” may tell you what the model lacks rather than what the program would do on Windows.</p>
<h3>A first experiment: catch the decoded URL</h3><p>Save your work, choose <strong>Load decoding demo</strong>, and inspect the prepared session. Add a write watchpoint around scratch at <code>0x60000000</code>. Run, inspect the first hit, then continue through the benign loop. Read scratch and capture 64 bytes into Recovery. Look for <code>https://decoded.example/config</code>. Return to Training ground and check the evidence. The demo replaces the active sample.</p>
<h3>Native function harness: call it with known inputs</h3><p>Find a function in Disassembly or Functions, prepare its start address in the debugger, then enable <strong>Call the selected entry as a native function</strong> under <strong>Experiment setup</strong>. Choose the calling convention that its caller actually uses. System V x64, Windows x64, x86 cdecl/stdcall, ARM/Thumb AAPCS32 and AArch64 AAPCS64 accept single-word integer or pointer arguments. RISC-V/MIPS still need manual register/stack setup. Floats, structs, multiword values and variadic calls are not inferred.</p><ol><li>Enter an Arguments JSON array such as <code>["2","3"]</code>. Use exact decimal or 0x strings. Values are unsigned bit patterns of the selected machine-word width.</li><li>For a pointer, enter <code>[{"buffer":"input"},"3"]</code> and put <code>[{"name":"input","hex":"41424300","size":32}]</code> in Input / output buffers. The named buffer contains ABC and a zero byte, padded to 32 bytes. Extra bytes start at zero.</li><li>Choose <strong>Initialize session</strong> to apply the setup. Run or step, then read <strong>Results &amp; observed coverage</strong>: return register, buffer addresses and before/after bytes.</li><li>Check the stop reason. A register at a fault or budget stop is intermediate state; it is not proof the function returned that value.</li></ol><p>System V x64 supplies RDI, RSI, RDX, RCX, R8 and R9, then stack arguments. Windows x64 supplies RCX, RDX, R8 and R9 plus shadow space and stack arguments. ARM uses R0–R3; AArch64 uses X0–X7. x86 supplies stack arguments. The synthetic stack has the selected ABI alignment; return goes to the lab boundary. In Windows mode, this function-entry option skips execution of TLS callbacks and process/DLL entry. If the function relies on that initialization, supply its state or investigate it separately.</p><p>Start with <strong>Load function + batch demo</strong>. It computes <code>left ? left + right : right</code>. With 2 and 3, Run returns 5. The demo replaces the current sample, so save your investigation first.</p>
<h3>Environment recipes: write down your assumptions</h3><p>Open <strong>Environment recipe · mapped memory, API hooks, virtual files and ARM MMIO</strong>. The editor takes JSON data; it does not evaluate scripts. The presets are examples to adapt, not automatic detection of what a target expects. Function arguments and buffers have their own controls above. The exported recipe combines them with the environment.</p><p>Use <code>files</code> for recorded virtual file contents, <code>registers</code> for general-register overrides, and <code>regions</code> for captured RAM. For example, <code>{"regions":[{"address":"0x20000000","size":4096,"permissions":"RW","hex":"414200","name":"Captured RAM"}]}</code> creates a zero-padded page with your initial bytes. Addresses and sizes are page aligned; maps cannot overlap existing memory. CPU writes into executable regions still stop. Register overrides apply after function arguments, so they can deliberately replace one.</p><p>A <code>hooks</code> record names one executable address or Windows <code>DLL!API</code>. It skips that function, reads arguments using your chosen ABI, supplies a return value, optionally writes output bytes, and returns to the caller. Example: <code>{"hooks":[{"api":"kernel32.dll!GetTickCount","abi":"win64","argc":0,"result":"1234","label":"Recorded clock value"}]}</code>. The log marks this as analyst-supplied. Unknown APIs still stop. This helps isolate a parser or replay a known response; it does not show what the real service would do.</p><p>For an output pointer, use <code>"writes":[{"argument":0,"offset":0,"hex":"44415441"}]</code> on the hook. It writes DATA to the address in argument zero. Set argc to include that argument. Outputs must be writable, non-executable mapped data; invalid targets stop. No hook reaches the network or host filesystem.</p><p><strong>Export recipe</strong> saves the sample hash and CPU setup. <strong>Import recipe</strong> checks that same sample and loads the recipe without running it. The full Notebook investigation saves the editors, experiment state and complete batch report. Edits apply on Initialize; Reset repeats the last initialized setup. Source and offline releases include <code>docs/EXPERIMENT-LAB.md</code> with the full schema, syscall table and examples.</p>
<h3>Linux: a small userspace environment you can inspect</h3><p>Choose <strong>Linux static ELF model</strong> for little-endian ELF32/ELF64 ET_EXEC on x86, x64, ARM/Thumb EABI5 or AArch64. It loads PT_LOAD bytes, zeroes BSS, and builds argc/argv/env and a small auxiliary vector at the real ELF entry. It rejects dynamic linking, interpreters, PIE/shared objects and TLS. This supports small static routines/programs that use its syscall subset; it does not boot Linux or promise to run an ordinary desktop binary.</p><p>A recipe can supply <code>"argv":["sample","--test"]</code>, <code>"env":["MODE=test"]</code>, <code>"stdin":"68690a"</code> and <code>"files":[{"path":"/config","hex":"41420a"}]</code>. Files are virtual buffers with absolute names and no .. path traversal. UID/GID 1000, PID 1337 and deterministic startup random bytes are visible model assumptions.</p><p>The supported syscall set includes read, write, readv/writev, uname, getcwd, identity queries, open (x86/x64/ARM/Thumb), openat with AT_FDCWD, close, lseek, anonymous private mmap without an address hint, mprotect/munmap for whole modeled allocations, bounded brk, getpid, exit and exit_group. Read/write and files are limited to 64 KiB at a time/per file. It does not implement sockets, pipes, threads, signals, a dynamic linker, a kernel or real device files. Unsupported numbers or argument variants stop; no invented success result is supplied.</p><ol><li>Select x64 or AArch64 and choose <strong>Load Linux demo</strong>.</li><li>Run it. The harmless program reads virtual stdin, writes the bytes to captured stdout, and exits zero.</li><li>Inspect Linux output and the syscall events. Raw stdout/stderr bytes remain in the exported report; displayed text may replace invalid UTF-8.</li><li>Change stdin in a batch case, run the cases, and compare the output. Files written by the interactive Linux session can be sent to Recovery lab.</li></ol>
<h3>ARM MMIO: give the routine a recorded hardware response</h3><p>MMIO means memory-mapped input/output: a memory address stands for a hardware register. A bare routine may poll one for a ready bit. SPECTRA can now supply explicit responses on ARM, Thumb and AArch64. It is not simulating the whole peripheral or board.</p><p>Example: <code>{"mmio":[{"address":"0x20000000","width":4,"values":["0","1"],"onExhaustion":"stop","write":"capture","label":"Recorded ready bit"}]}</code>. Reads consume 0, then 1. The next read stops unless you explicitly set onExhaustion to hold, which repeats the final value. Captured writes are evidence only: they do not trigger side effects or change the read sequence. Read width must match an aligned 1, 2 or 4-byte record exactly.</p><ol><li>Use <strong>Load ARM register demo</strong> for a ready-made experiment.</li><li>Run it and open <strong>Recipe, Linux and hardware events</strong>.</li><li>Find the supplied read value 41 and captured write value 42. The three instructions read, increment and write back; the fourth returns.</li><li>Run the prepared cases with status 41 and 99. Compare the captured outputs while keeping the rest of the setup fixed.</li></ol><p>Unknown register addresses, widths, forbidden writes and exhausted sequences stop. The last CPU operation can be partial, so it is excluded from coverage. Map captured RAM under regions, independently of MMIO pages. There is no interrupt controller, timing model, DMA, boot flow or full device behavior; MMIO recipes are separate from Linux process mode.</p>
<h3>Coverage and input batches: which path did these inputs take?</h3><p><strong>Observed instruction locations &amp; transitions</strong> records where completed operations went and their hit counts. Explicit modeled hook/syscall boundaries are included and labeled in events. This is not a whole-binary coverage percentage. An unvisited branch might simply need a different input or environment. Partial operations are excluded. The report retains up to 8,192 locations and 8,192 transitions; the page shows the first 256 locations and 100 transitions. Clicking a location reads the current interactive session's bytes, which may differ from a batch case's bytes.</p><ol><li>Initialize the desired CPU, entry and mapping.</li><li>Enter a Cases JSON array, such as <code>[{"label":"zero","arguments":["0","3"]},{"label":"seven","arguments":["7","3"]}]</code>.</li><li>Choose <strong>Run cases</strong>. Each uses a fresh emulator, the current recipe, and its overrides. The interactive session's register/memory edits, breakpoints and watchpoints are not inherited.</li><li>Inspect each stop reason, instruction count, return value and changed buffers. Added/missing locations compare against the first case whose setup succeeded. A faulting baseline remains a faulting baseline.</li><li>Click a case name to inspect its buffers, Linux output, modeled events and coverage. Choose <strong>Show interactive result</strong> to return to your live session's evidence.</li><li><strong>Export batch report</strong> or save the full investigation in Notebook.</li></ol><p>A case may replace arguments, buffers, regions, hooks, mmio, files, argv, env or stdin; register overrides merge by name. Limits are 16 cases, 10,000 instructions each from Run budget, and 30 seconds overall. Pause cancels the batch; an incomplete batch does not replace the previous complete report. Setup errors stay in the results and later cases still run. Imported reports are data, not proof that the reported execution occurred.</p><p>Saved checkpoints now verify recipes, mapped state, Linux files and descriptors, MMIO sequence positions, buffers and coverage alongside CPU state. They still require the same engine fingerprint and sample. Use the original offline release to replay a checkpoint from an older build; changing its engine label will not make it compatible.</p>
<h3>FAQ / hardware and limits</h3><dl><dt>My routine faults on a read.</dt><dd>Check the address, pointer argument, length, stack setup, and mapped region. A missing object or dependency is not fixed by blindly skipping the instruction.</dd><dt>Can I emulate an entire firmware board?</dt><dd>No. ARM/Thumb/AArch64, RISC-V, and MIPS paths cover tested integer routine profiles. Exact-width analyst MMIO responses are available on ARM/Thumb/AArch64; whole peripherals, OS/RTOS boot, FP/SIMD, and many extensions are not modeled. Unsupported operations stop.</dd><dt>Why is the execution trace shorter than the run?</dt><dd>The table retains the latest 200 attempts, newest first. Export relevant evidence and use breakpoints/checkpoints to keep experiments focused.</dd><dt>What about real native process debugging?</dt><dd>The workflow stays in the browser. Process models provide selected loader and API/syscall behavior, not a complete OS. Stepping, breakpoints, registers, live memory maps, output and traces stay in this workspace. Dynamic linking, threads and missing system behavior are still real limits.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Capture interesting bytes into Recovery, check the tested condition in Decompile, and save inputs, stop reason, trace, and interpretation in Notebook. State what your model did and what still needs a real controlled test.</p>`},
{id:'functions',title:'Functions',view:'functions',summary:'Recovered entry points, callers, evidence sources, and meaningful names.',body:`
<h3>Library-function evidence</h3><p>Below the function list, inspect declared library names, import-forwarding thunks and exact byte candidates. These are different kinds of evidence. An import slot tells you what was requested; an exact function match tells you the bytes match a reference. Neither proves the file is harmless or identifies the original author.</p><p>The starter corpus contains 30 x86/x64 builds of small Church memory/string implementations. It is intentionally described as reference candidates, not a complete libc database. Ambiguous names stay separate. <strong>Use this name</strong> saves your choice and its supporting evidence in Notebook; it also marks old reconstructed code for refresh.</p><ol><li>Select a function in Disassembly and review its boundaries and behavior.</li><li>In Functions, give its signature a meaningful name and choose Export selected function signature.</li><li>Import a reviewed signature pack when inspecting another build. It stays local and saves with the investigation.</li></ol><p>Exact signatures need a complete contiguous function of 16–4096 bytes. Relocations, compiler changes or gaps can prevent a match. Packs can describe any supported decoder family, with at most 512 entries and 256 KiB of signature bytes; no scripts or wildcard rules run. No match means unknown. Try Load analysis demo to inspect real matches with included source.</p>
<h3>Why functions matter</h3><p>A function is a useful unit for asking what a piece of code accepts, computes, and returns. The Functions view is an index into SPECTRA’s decoded code. It is not guaranteed to be the original source-level function list: stripped names, inlining, tail calls, wrappers, and indirect control flow complicate recovery.</p>
<h3>Read each column</h3><dl><dt>Name and address</dt><dd>A recovered symbol, generated name, or your annotation and its start VA. A name like <code>sub_401000</code> describes an address, not the function’s purpose.</dd><dt>Instruction count</dt><dd>Instructions associated through the bounded reachability pass. It is not a count of all possible runtime operations.</dd><dt>Callers</dt><dd>Known decoded call references to the start. Unresolved indirect calls are not an exhaustive caller list.</dd><dt>Evidence/source</dt><dd>Why the start was considered: entry point, symbols, direct calls, supported unwind metadata, or another recovered seed.</dd><dt>Boundary</dt><dd>A metadata size when available, otherwise inferred reachability. Neither justifies assuming neighboring bytes belong to the same source function.</dd></dl>
<h3>Use the index</h3><ol><li>Choose <strong>Analyze code</strong> if the list has not been built.</li><li>Search by name, address, or evidence text. Start from a meaningful symbol or a function reached from a string/import lead.</li><li>Click a function to open its code. Read its beginning, calls, branches, and return behavior.</li><li>Open <strong>Name / annotate</strong> and give it a working name such as <code>parse_header_candidate</code>. Keep the uncertainty visible when you have not proven its role.</li><li>Use the selected function in Decompile or prepare it in Debugger lab. Record the caller’s argument setup before trying to emulate it.</li></ol>
<h3>A productive way to name things</h3><p>Prefer observable work over a dramatic guess. “Compares the first four bytes with this constant” is better evidence than “malware checker.” A short name plus a comment can capture both: <code>check_header_magic</code>, followed by the bytes, address, and callers you checked. Renames follow the address through supported workspace views.</p>
<h3>FAQ / incomplete recovery</h3><dl><dt>Why is the function I expected missing?</dt><dd>It may be inlined, only reached indirectly, outside decoded ranges, packed, or absent from available metadata. Use a justified raw range or investigate a known code address.</dd><dt>Does zero callers mean dead code?</dt><dd>No. Entry points, callbacks, virtual dispatch, exports, or unresolved pointers may reach it.</dd><dt>Why are there strange tiny functions?</dt><dd>They can be real wrappers/thunks or recovery artifacts. Inspect the instructions and source of the start before merging their meaning into your analysis.</dd><dt>Can I use this instead of the .NET tree?</dt><dd>Use Decompile’s Types &amp; methods for managed assemblies. That view understands managed metadata and supports harness selection.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Open Disassembly to see the bytes and call relationships; use Decompile for a working high-level explanation. Keep your names, comments, and signature hypotheses in Notebook.</p>`},
{id:'references',title:'Cross-references',view:'references',summary:'Find who points where, distinguish calls from address clues, and trace evidence.',body:`
<h3>Read the newer evidence kinds</h3><p>The kind filter includes indirect calls, indirect branches and switch cases. Follow the source instruction and read its evidence. Register-built constants, file-pointer candidates and analyst hypotheses are labeled separately. Table references retain case labels, entry offsets and raw values in exported code JSON. A renamed destination follows its address here; the evidence remains attached to the original instruction bytes.</p>
<h3>The connections between discoveries</h3><p>A cross-reference, or xref, records a relationship from one code location to another address. It helps answer “who uses this string?”, “who calls this routine?”, and “what does this instruction point at?” A static xref is evidence that the decoded instruction contains a relationship; it is not a runtime trace.</p>
<h3>Reading the list</h3><p>The source address identifies the referring instruction. The kind describes the relationship. The destination VA and destination description point to code, a string, an import slot, mapped bytes, or an unresolved/unmapped target. The evidence column explains how SPECTRA derived the link.</p><dl><dt>Calls and branches</dt><dd>Resolved control-flow targets. A conditional branch has more than one possible path; a listed target alone does not say the branch is taken.</dd><dt>Import slots</dt><dd>References associated with a parsed external-symbol slot. The runtime value of that slot may differ from its static label.</dd><dt>Address operands / memory operands</dt><dd>Decoded operands that can be related to an address. An address calculation can prepare a pointer without reading the pointed-to data.</dd><dt>Address constants</dt><dd>Values that look like addresses in the analyzed context. Treat these as candidates until the surrounding operations establish a purpose.</dd></dl>
<h3>Follow one string into its caller</h3><ol><li>Select the string in Strings and choose <strong>Find code references</strong>. Let code analysis finish if needed.</li><li>Read the context heading: you may be viewing only references for that string or a function.</li><li>Click the source address to open the instruction. Inspect the neighboring argument setup and the call that follows.</li><li>Follow the destination or related function. If the reference points to bytes, confirm their location in Hex.</li><li>Use <strong>Show all references</strong> to leave the current context, then filter by kind or search text/address for the next question.</li></ol>
<h3>How this helps beyond malware</h3><p>For a thick client, a request path can lead to its request builder and serializer. For firmware, a log message can lead to a peripheral-handling routine. For a patch review, a changed error string can lead to the newly added check. The useful part is the connection between the bytes and their users.</p>
<h3>FAQ / missing or misleading links</h3><dl><dt>No references were found. Is the string unused?</dt><dd>That is one possibility, not a finding. Address arithmetic, tables, indirect loads, different base assumptions, runtime construction, or undecoded code can hide the relationship.</dd><dt>The target is unmapped.</dt><dd>Check the file’s mapping and your raw load base. It can also refer to runtime memory or external state that the static file does not contain.</dd><dt>Does every call label resolve the real callee?</dt><dd>No. Indirect calls may name an import slot or remain unresolved. Inspect how the function pointer is filled.</dd><dt>Why did a filter remove everything?</dt><dd>A string/function context and a kind/text filter can both apply. Show all references and clear the filter before assuming no result exists.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Disassembly supplies instruction context; Functions organizes the callers; Decompile explains the logic. Save the source and target addresses together in Notebook so someone else can reproduce the connection.</p>`},
{id:'hex',title:'Hex explorer',view:'hex',summary:'Ground truth in file bytes, offsets, ASCII, and mapping mistakes.',body:`
<h3>The bytes behind every interpretation</h3><p>Hex explorer shows the original file as hexadecimal byte values and a printable ASCII column. It is the place to verify a parser’s claim, inspect a header or boundary, or check the bytes behind a string or instruction. This view does not edit the file and does not show the debugger’s changing memory.</p>
<h3>Read a row</h3><p>The left value is the file offset of the row’s first byte. Columns <code>00</code> through <code>0F</code> are the next 16 positions. Add the column number to the row offset to locate one byte. The right column renders printable ASCII; other values appear as dots. A dot is a display choice, not necessarily a zero byte.</p><p>For a row at <code>0x120</code>, column <code>0A</code> is offset <code>0x12A</code>. Bytes <code>48 69 00</code> are <code>H</code>, <code>i</code>, and a zero byte. Four bytes <code>78 56 34 12</code> interpreted as a little-endian 32-bit integer represent <code>0x12345678</code>; the byte display itself does not choose a data type for you.</p>
<h3>Navigate deliberately</h3><ol><li>Arrive from <strong>View in hex</strong> on a string, <strong>View bytes</strong> on an instruction, a section/import offset, or the entropy landscape.</li><li>Enter a <strong>File offset</strong> in decimal or <code>0x</code> notation and choose <strong>Go</strong>. Navigation aligns the view to the containing 16-byte row.</li><li>Use Previous/Next to page through 256-byte windows. Check the displayed range and total file size.</li><li>Read the selected-string context and highlighting when present. Verify its start, size, and terminator rather than relying only on the preview.</li></ol>
<h3>Questions Hex can settle</h3><p>Does the supposed Base64 text really occupy this range? Is there a header immediately before an embedded container? Did the disassembler read the bytes you intended? Is a reported zero-filled region actually present on disk? Those are narrower, answerable questions that help prevent bigger mistakes.</p>
<h3>FAQ / wrong address space</h3><dl><dt>Why can’t I go to the address from the debugger?</dt><dd>The debugger uses VAs. Convert through the relevant mapping to a file offset if file-backed bytes exist. Heap, stack, scratch, and zero-filled memory may have no disk position.</dd><dt>Why didn’t a debugger patch appear here?</dt><dd>It changed the emulator’s session memory. Hex still shows the original file.</dd><dt>Can I select an arbitrary range and patch the binary?</dt><dd>This release’s Hex view is an inspector. Recovery has explicit offset/length transforms; Debugger lab has separate session-only patches.</dd><dt>The ASCII column looks empty. Is the region encrypted?</dt><dd>Maybe, but code, images, compressed data, and many normal binary formats also look unreadable. Check structure, entropy, and contextual evidence.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Use File structure to understand mapping, Disassembly for a justified code range, Recovery to transform selected bytes, or Firmware files to inspect a container header at this offset.</p>`},
{id:'structure',title:'File structure',view:'structure',summary:'Identity, sections, imports, entry points, and the shape of a binary.',body:`
<h3>Read the blueprint before following a lead</h3><p>File structure parses supported PE and ELF metadata. It gives context for the rest of the workbench: which CPU to use, how file regions map into memory, where execution may begin, and what external functions are declared. Strings and Hex still work when a file is not a supported executable.</p>
<h3>File identity</h3><dl><dt>Format and architecture</dt><dd>PE often appears in Windows executables/libraries; ELF appears in many Linux, Unix-like, and embedded targets. Format alone does not tell you whether the contents are native, managed, packed, or trustworthy.</dd><dt>Address width</dt><dd>The parsed image’s 32/64-bit address model. Choose compatible decoder/emulator settings; host laptop bitness is not the target’s bitness.</dd><dt>Entry point (VA)</dt><dd>A declared startup location. It may lead to a loader, runtime startup, or unpacking stub rather than the interesting application logic.</dd><dt>Image base</dt><dd>A mapping reference used to interpret relative addresses. Runtime rebasing can change actual addresses.</dd><dt>File size and SHA-256</dt><dd>Identify the artifact being discussed. Compare exact hashes before assuming two copies are identical.</dd></dl>
<h3>Sections and permissions</h3><p>Each section row shows a name, permissions, size, file position, mapped address, and entropy. Click its name to inspect the bytes. Names like <code>.text</code> and <code>.data</code> are conventions, not guarantees. Read/write/execute flags express the parsed mapping; they do not prove every byte in an executable region is code.</p><p>High entropy suggests a varied byte distribution. Compression, encryption, and ordinary media can all produce it. Writable-and-executable regions or unusual section names deserve context, not an automatic malware verdict. Check the entry region, imports, and Detect findings together.</p>
<h3>Imported functions</h3><p>Imports describe declared external functionality. SPECTRA shows the library, function, parsed table offset, and any API clue. Click the table offset to see the underlying file bytes. PE import slots and supported ELF symbols/relocations help code analysis connect indirect references to names.</p><p>A networking or crypto import means the binary references that capability; it does not establish which call path runs or what arguments are used. Statically linked code, dynamic symbol lookup, packing, and custom resolvers can leave little visible import information.</p>
<h3>A useful structure pass</h3><ol><li>Write format, CPU, hash, and entry VA in Notebook.</li><li>Locate the entry’s mapping and inspect unusual sections in Hex.</li><li>Pick one relevant imported function and look for code references or surrounding call logic.</li><li>Use Detect to check toolchain/packing clues, then select the right decompiler path.</li></ol>
<h3>FAQ</h3><dl><dt>No sections or imports were parsed.</dt><dd>The file may be raw data, a container, unsupported, truncated, or intentionally malformed. Read the warning banner. Do not force a PE/ELF interpretation onto arbitrary bytes.</dd><dt>Is a missing import evidence that the program cannot network?</dt><dd>No. It can use dynamic lookup, a library, direct system interfaces, or code not visible in the parsed subset.</dd><dt>Why do file offsets and VAs differ?</dt><dd>Headers describe loading relationships. File padding, aligned sections, rebasing, and memory-only regions make the two coordinate systems different.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Detect helps identify the toolchain; Disassembly follows the entry or chosen function; Firmware files handles supported containers; Hex lets you verify every mapped-file claim.</p>`},
{id:'detect',title:'Detect',view:'detect',summary:'Real DiE fingerprints, heuristic labels, raw evidence, and scan limits.',body:`
<h3>Ask what built it and what might wrap it</h3><p>Detect runs the real Detect It Easy (DiE) 3.21 scanner with its matching pinned database inside a local browser worker. It can report compiler/language, linker, library, operating-system, debug-data, packer, protector, and obfuscator fingerprints. The browser integration is credited in the panel. This release does not merge ExeInfoPE or claim to contain every current signature.</p>
<h3>Run a scan</h3><ol><li>Open the sample and wait for basic inspection to finish. DiE’s sample cap is 16 MiB, smaller than the general workbench limit.</li><li>Leave <strong>Heuristics</strong> on for the default scan, or turn it off for a more restricted pass. <strong>Deep scan</strong> optionally looks further for embedded-file evidence; it is not full unpacking.</li><li>Choose <strong>Scan with DiE</strong>. About 20 MiB of runtime assets download on first use. Keep the tab active while the scan works.</li><li>Read the status and cards. Use <strong>Cancel</strong> if you want to discard the running scanner. The whole job has a 180-second boundary, including loading.</li><li>Open <strong>Raw DiE report</strong> and <strong>Export report</strong> when you need the exact upstream result and scan context.</li></ol>
<h3>Interpret a finding</h3><p>Cards retain category, name/version, details, format/region, and offset. <strong>Database detection</strong> and <strong>Heuristic</strong> remain distinct. Expand <strong>What does this tell me?</strong> for a plain explanation and the original display text. Provenance includes sample SHA-256, engine version, and scan options.</p><p>A UPX match gives you a known-packer lead. It does not unpack the file or show the original program’s logic. An MSIL/C# and .NET Core match suggests trying ILSpy. A crypto-related library or protector label does not prove malicious use. The scanner’s printed version strings are evidence from the engine, not independently verified build records.</p>
<h3>Privacy and resource use</h3><p>The scanner runs inside a fresh browser-local VM with guest networking disabled. It receives the file under a fixed internal name for inspection; it does not launch that file. Cancel/completion discards the scanner. The guest has 128 MiB of RAM plus browser/engine overhead. Slow devices or large/deep scans may reach the time limit.</p>
<h3>FAQ / expected limits</h3><dl><dt>No match. Is the file unpacked?</dt><dd>No. An unfamiliar packer, transformed stub, unsupported format, or changed build can evade identification. Compare with structure and code evidence.</dd><dt>Two labels seem to disagree.</dt><dd>Mixed-language builds, runtimes, nested content, copied signatures, or heuristic mistakes can explain that. Keep both raw findings and investigate their regions.</dd><dt>Why did the scan stop?</dt><dd>Check the displayed error. Loading, scan complexity, the file cap, or the deadline can stop it. Try heuristics/deep scan off or a smaller meaningful artifact; a failed scan is not a clean bill of health.</dd><dt>Do findings survive closing the tab?</dt><dd>They are included in a saved full investigation and bound to the sample hash. Use Notebook’s full save/export, not just a string report.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Use the label to choose a method: ILSpy for managed code, Ghidra for supported native code, Recovery for encoded/compressed layers, or a small emulator experiment for runtime-built data. Verify the interesting claim in bytes or code.</p>`},
{id:'recovery',title:'Recovery lab',view:'recovery',summary:'Candidate hunts, stack writes, keyed transforms, provenance, and decoded layers.',body:`
<h3>Recover something you can inspect</h3><p>Recovery lab works on byte ranges and records where results came from. It can hunt encoded candidates, recognize bounded x86 stack-write strings, apply a known transform/key, and receive captured emulator memory. It is not a general solution for encrypted binaries or whole-program unpacking.</p>
<h3>Choose the input before the offset</h3><p><strong>Input</strong> selects the original file or a recovered layer. <strong>Offset</strong> and <strong>Byte length</strong> refer to that selected input, not always to the original file. If layer 2 came from decoding a blob at original offset <code>0x900</code>, offset <code>0</code> in layer 2 means its first decoded byte. Keep the transform chain when relating the two.</p>
<h3>Hunt encoded strings and inspect candidates</h3><ol><li>Select a focused byte range with a reason to inspect it: a string reference, configuration region, or a compression clue.</li><li>Choose <strong>Hunt encoded strings</strong>. The bounded hunt includes Base64/hex candidates, one-byte XOR candidates, and packing/compression clues.</li><li>Filter the result list by method or search its text/offset. Read the method, source, and confidence rather than ranking solely by how readable a result looks.</li><li>Select a candidate to inspect its source preview, decoded offset where available, method, key evidence, and contributing instructions.</li><li>Choose <strong>Save evidence to notebook</strong> for a useful result. Use <strong>Source bytes</strong> when the source is the original file.</li></ol><p>XOR searches can produce accidental words. Check whether nearby bytes decode coherently and whether code uses that key and range. A scoring label is a triage aid, not statistical proof.</p>
<h3>Recover stack strings</h3><p><strong>Recover stack strings</strong> uses supported x86/x64 instruction patterns to track immediate writes that build readable text in stack-like locations. It can explain strings that do not exist as one contiguous text run in the file. Inspect the contributing instructions. This is bounded static tracking, not universal symbolic execution or a full run of the program; unsupported transformations can break the chain.</p>
<h3>Decode bytes with a known format or key</h3><dl><dt>Repeating XOR</dt><dd>Supply key bytes in hex. Key position starts at the first selected byte. Choosing a different range start changes the phase of a multi-byte key.</dd><dt>RC4</dt><dd>Uses standard RC4 from the start of the keystream. A target with a dropped prefix or warm-up step needs a matching analysis outside this simple transform.</dd><dt>AES-CBC</dt><dd>Supply the actual AES key and 16-byte IV; this path expects PKCS#7 padding. A wrong key/range or different padding scheme can fail.</dd><dt>AES-GCM</dt><dd>Supply the actual key, 12-byte nonce, optional AAD, and ciphertext with the trailing 16-byte authentication tag. Authentication failure means some required input/layout may be wrong; it is not permission to ignore the tag.</dd><dt>Gzip, Zlib / DEFLATE, Raw DEFLATE</dt><dd>Choose the wrapper that really surrounds the compressed stream. Expansion gives you data bytes; it does not reconstruct an executable loader’s runtime behavior.</dd><dt>Base64 / Hex text</dt><dd>Decode the selected file bytes as the stated textual encoding. For one copied text value, Decoder lab is often faster.</dd></dl><p>Enter offset, length, format and any key/IV/AAD, then choose <strong>Decode selected bytes</strong>. Encryption still needs the correct key and parameters. SPECTRA does not infer arbitrary cryptographic keys.</p>
<h3>Decoded layers and emulator captures</h3><p>Each layer keeps bytes, size, extracted strings, entropy, hash, and source/transform provenance. <strong>Use as next layer</strong> makes it the next input without replacing the current sample. <strong>Save decoded bytes</strong> downloads that layer; save your project before opening it as a new sample. Bytes captured by Debugger lab also arrive here with the experiment’s assumptions.</p><p><strong>Export recovery report</strong> keeps findings and metadata; use a full Notebook project or save the layer bytes when you need the actual data. Supplied transform keys are not included in the recovery report; derived one-byte XOR keys are. Record needed reproduction parameters deliberately in your own protected notes.</p>
<h3>FAQ / boundaries</h3><dl><dt>Why did a transform refuse the range?</dt><dd>Transform input is capped at 4 MiB, output at 8 MiB, with eight layers / 16 MiB total and a worker deadline. Narrow the range or save/export and clear old results.</dd><dt>A decoded sentence appeared. Is that the real configuration?</dt><dd>Check its source, key, complete layout, and code references. Plausible plaintext alone does not establish runtime use.</dd><dt>Can it unpack any protected executable?</dt><dd>No. These are particular recovery techniques. A custom loader may require a specific routine, environment, or additional tools.</dd><dt>What does Clear results do?</dt><dd>It removes the current recovery findings/layers. Save anything you need first. Cancel only stops the current job.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Inspect recovered values in their code context, chain another justified transform, or save bytes and open a child analysis. Notebook is where the source hash, range, parameters, and conclusion stay together.</p>`},
{id:'decoder',title:'Decoder lab',view:'decoder',summary:'Quick text transforms, encoding versus encryption, and checking the output.',body:`
<h3>A small workbench for one value</h3><p>Decoder lab transforms pasted text or a selected string sent from Strings. It is for quick checks such as “is this Base64?” or “what does this percent-encoded path say?” For large file ranges, binary output, cryptographic parameters, or preserved transform chains, use Recovery lab.</p>
<h3>Use it</h3><ol><li>Choose <strong>Decode</strong> in the String inspector or paste a value into <strong>Input</strong>.</li><li>Select the transform. For XOR, supply the hex key.</li><li>Choose <strong>Run transform</strong> and inspect <strong>Output</strong>.</li><li>Use <strong>Copy</strong> when needed, and write the original offset, transform, and result in Notebook if the finding matters. The scratch text fields are not a substitute for a saved recovery layer.</li></ol>
<h3>The four transforms</h3><dl><dt>Base64 → text</dt><dd>Base64 represents bytes with printable characters; it is an encoding, not encryption. The text-oriented display is useful for a message or URL. If the output is binary or garbled, inspect it through Recovery’s byte layers.</dd><dt>Hex → UTF-8 text</dt><dd>Turns hex byte pairs into bytes, then displays text. Try <code>68 65 6c 6c 6f</code> to get <code>hello</code>. Odd or invalid hex input needs correction first.</dd><dt>URL decode</dt><dd>Decodes percent-escaped characters. Try <code>%2Fapi%2Fstatus</code> to get <code>/api/status</code>. This transforms text; it does not visit a URL.</dd><dt>UTF-8 text XOR → text</dt><dd>XORs the input’s UTF-8 bytes with a repeating hex key. This is not the same as saying “my input is a string of hex ciphertext.” Use Recovery for byte-range transforms when representation matters.</dd></dl>
<h3>Try a harmless chain</h3><p>Decode <code>aGVsbG8=</code> as Base64. You should see <code>hello</code>. Now think about a real sample: you still need its original location and the code that decodes or uses it. Multiple layers are possible, but repeatedly trying transforms until a few printable characters appear is not a reliable conclusion.</p>
<h3>FAQ / mistakes that waste time</h3><dl><dt>Why does the output contain replacement characters?</dt><dd>The decoded bytes may not be valid UTF-8 text. That can be normal for binary data, compression, or ciphertext. Switch to a byte-oriented Recovery layer.</dd><dt>Base64 failed. Is it encrypted?</dt><dd>Not necessarily. Padding, an alternate alphabet, missing bytes, delimiters, or a false positive can explain the failure. Inspect the actual stored representation.</dd><dt>Will it brute-force AES or recover passwords?</dt><dd>No. These controls perform the stated transforms. Recovery’s keyed cryptographic transforms still require correct parameters.</dd><dt>Where did the decoded value go?</dt><dd>It stays in the scratch output until replaced or the page is reloaded. Copy useful results and document them; save byte-backed layers through Recovery when provenance matters.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Return to the original string and follow its references, or use Recovery for a repeatable byte-level transformation. A decoded value becomes useful when you can explain who consumes it.</p>`},
{id:'compare',title:'Binary diff',view:'compare',summary:'Two versions, match confidence, changed strings/imports, and patch reasoning.',body:`
<h3>Compare changes, then explain them</h3><p>Binary diff helps compare two builds: a vendor update, a patch candidate, or two versions of your own program. It looks at strings, imports, and decoded functions. It distinguishes exact decoded-byte matches from candidate matches by symbol or shape. It does not prove two functions are behaviorally equivalent or that a security issue is fixed.</p>
<h3>Run the comparison</h3><ol><li>Open the earlier version as the main SPECTRA sample. Save any investigation you need before replacing it.</li><li>Choose <strong>Open second binary</strong> for the later version. Both files remain local; each must be at most 32 MiB.</li><li>Choose <strong>Compare</strong>. Read the before/after filenames, hashes, and warnings in the summary.</li><li>Inspect <strong>Matched functions</strong>: name pair, match basis, confidence, and whether decoded bytes changed.</li><li>Select a pair to see assembly side by side. Choose <strong>Decompile selected pair</strong> for supported native reconstructions, loaded sequentially.</li><li>Open <strong>Unmatched functions &amp; ambiguous groups</strong>, plus changed Strings and Imports. Export the comparison and record the specific hypothesis you checked.</li></ol>
<h3>Exact, candidate, unmatched</h3><p>An exact match is equality of the compared decoded instruction bytes for those recovered functions. It is stronger than a similar name or instruction shape, but identical code can still behave differently if data, callees, imports, or environment changed. Candidate matches need manual confirmation. Unmatched does not necessarily mean added/deleted source code: optimization, layout changes, or incomplete recovery can prevent alignment.</p><p>Ambiguous groups mean several functions share the evidence used for matching. Keeping that uncertainty visible is better than inventing a one-to-one answer. The function pass and on-screen tables are bounded; use the exported report and warning text to understand coverage.</p>
<h3>A patch-verification example</h3><p>Suppose the later build adds a length check before copying a buffer. Follow the changed function, compare both conditions and their failure paths, and trace the value supplying the length. Then check whether every relevant caller reaches the new check. A new error string is a lead; the actual guarded operation and reachable paths establish the stronger finding.</p>
<h3>Raw code and limitations</h3><p>If the comparison uses a current raw disassembly setup, the same selected range/mapping assumptions are applied to both sides. That is only appropriate when both layouts fit. A shifted image may need separate manual investigation rather than pretending the same offsets mean the same function.</p>
<h3>FAQ</h3><dl><dt>Everything looks changed after a rebuild.</dt><dd>Compiler settings, optimization, addresses, embedded metadata, and function layout can change many bytes without the same amount of logic change. Start with symbols and a focused question.</dd><dt>The strings list is huge or clipped.</dt><dd>The UI shows a bounded preview of computed additions/removals. Export the report for the calculated set; it is still limited by extraction and comparison budgets.</dd><dt>Can it compare managed source semantically?</dt><dd>This release’s comparison is byte/metadata/decoded-function based. The selected-pair reconstruction is native; do not read it as a full .NET semantic diff engine.</dd><dt>Will the second file survive a saved project?</dt><dd>A full investigation can retain it and recompute comparison evidence on restore. This makes the project larger and includes those bytes even if the main sample is omitted.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Inspect a changed path in Disassembly/Decompile, use a bounded experiment where useful, and keep both hashes, match basis, and unresolved questions in Notebook.</p>`},
{id:'firmware',title:'Firmware files',view:'firmware',summary:'Bounded container inspection, child artifacts, offsets, and extraction provenance.',body:`
<h3>Firmware often contains other files</h3><p>A firmware image can combine boot code, filesystems, archives, compressed data, configuration, and vendor headers. Firmware files handles a supported subset of containers and exposes regular-file members as byte buffers. It is not a universal filesystem mounter or automatic firmware boot environment.</p>
<h3>Inspect a container</h3><ol><li>Open the image. Check its structure, strings, and Hex for a reason to expect a container.</li><li>Set <strong>Container file offset</strong>. Use zero for a container starting at the beginning, or a known embedded header offset.</li><li>Choose <strong>Inspect container</strong>. Read the detected format, wrapper, entry count, expanded size, and note.</li><li>Inspect each entry’s name, kind, size, and offset. Links/devices and other non-regular entries stay metadata.</li><li>Choose <strong>Download bytes</strong> to preserve a regular member or <strong>Open child investigation</strong> to analyze it in SPECTRA.</li></ol>
<h3>Supported formats and boundaries</h3><p>This release handles supported ZIP stored/deflated members, USTAR/PAX tar, newc/crc CPIO, and gzip wrappers. SquashFS 4.0 little-endian images support gzip, XZ and Zstandard, including metadata blocks, file blocks, sparse data and fragment tails. Older/big-endian SquashFS, LZMA, LZO and LZ4 are not supported. Unsupported features stop with a message; no guessed extraction is shown.</p><p>Extraction is bounded to a 64 MiB expanded container, 16 MiB regular member, 2,000 entries, and a 30-second job. Nested extraction is manual: inspect a member, then inspect the next container inside it if appropriate. Hitting a limit is not evidence that the file is corrupt.</p>
<h3>Read and export the inventory</h3><p>Regular files show a basic format/architecture identity when recognized. <strong>Export file inventory</strong> keeps paths, kinds, sizes, hashes and provenance without embedding member bytes. It is a file inventory, not a verified package SBOM or a verdict about vulnerable software.</p><p>For SquashFS, the displayed container position identifies the inode metadata block, with the inode offset retained in the report. It is not the start of the decompressed file’s bytes: file data may span compressed blocks and shared fragments. Symlinks, devices and special entries stay metadata; only regular byte buffers can open as child investigations.</p>
<h3>Parent, child, and the offset trap</h3><p>Opening a child replaces the active sample. Export/save the parent investigation first. The child’s Notebook starts with the parent hash/name, container format/wrapper, container offset, member path/offset/size, and the child’s hash. Preserve those notes so another researcher can get back to the same artifact.</p><p>When a wrapper has been decompressed, an “expanded” offset belongs to the expanded data. You cannot add it directly to the original compressed file offset and claim that is the byte’s disk location. Compression changes the relationship between positions.</p>
<h3>Useful questions after extraction</h3><p>A text configuration might explain a firmware service’s defaults. A shared library might contain the request parser. A certificate bundle might reveal expected trust roots. A nested executable might need ARM/MIPS/RISC-V analysis. Choose the tool based on the extracted member’s own format and evidence, not only on the outer firmware’s label.</p>
<h3>FAQ</h3><dl><dt>Why does an entry say Metadata only?</dt><dd>It is not an exposed regular-file byte buffer. SPECTRA does not follow links or create special devices from the archive.</dd><dt>Can I open an empty member?</dt><dd>There are no bytes to analyze. Download it if the empty artifact itself matters.</dd><dt>Why does Inspect container fail at zero?</dt><dd>A vendor header or another region may precede the container, or the format is unsupported. Find a justified offset; random scanning is not a substitute for understanding the image.</dd><dt>Does extracting a program run it?</dt><dd>No. It becomes a local byte buffer or downloaded artifact. Opening the child in SPECTRA performs static analysis.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Use File structure and Detect on the child, Strings for configuration/error clues, and native Decompile/Debugger settings appropriate to its CPU. Keep the parent-to-child chain in Notebook.</p>`},
{id:'lessons',title:'Training ground',view:'lessons',summary:'The reference book, benign exercises, evidence checks, sources, and offline releases.',body:`
<h3>Use the same tools while learning</h3><p>Training ground combines this field guide with small benign exercises. Each exercise gives you a concrete observation to chase in the normal workbench. You can keep explanations on while learning and turn them off later; the tools themselves stay available.</p>
<h3>The field guide</h3><p>Search the chapter list by a tool, control, or concept such as “watchpoint,” “offset,” or “harness.” Choose a chapter, read its steps and FAQ, and use <strong>Open this section</strong> to return to the workbench without loading a different sample. Previous/Next walks through the book. <strong>Download field guide</strong> saves a standalone HTML copy; open that copy in a browser to read offline or use the browser’s Print / Save as PDF command.</p>
<h3>Exercise 1: a name in the bytes</h3><ol><li>Save the current investigation, then choose <strong>Load the ELF exercise</strong>.</li><li>Find <code>SPECTRA_FIXTURE_ONLY.example</code> in Strings and ask for code references.</li><li>Follow the instruction into its caller and inspect the call to <code>fixture_helper</code>. Determine what value the helper reads and returns.</li><li>Return here and choose <strong>Check my evidence</strong>. The check looks for a decoded reference tied to this exercise’s sample hash.</li></ol><p>The helper returns the first byte of the marker, decimal 83. Use the Hint and Walkthrough to recover when stuck, then verify the result yourself.</p>
<h3>Exercise 2: decoded at runtime</h3><ol><li>Choose <strong>Load the decoding exercise</strong>. It prepares the benign XOR loop in Debugger lab.</li><li>Step, watch a scratch-memory write, and run through the loop.</li><li>Capture the scratch bytes into Recovery and inspect the decoded URL.</li><li>Return for the evidence check, which looks for the emulated-memory result.</li></ol><p>This exercise connects code, changing state, recovered text, and provenance. Seeing the expected string teaches one small mechanism; it does not mean every runtime string decoder works the same way.</p>
<h3>Exercise 3: hardware routines</h3><p>Select ARM32, Thumb, AArch64, RISC-V 32/64+C, or MIPS32/64 and load the exercise. The tiny raw routine is mapped at <code>0x1000</code>. Initialize, then set its two argument registers to 2 and 3: <code>R0/R1</code> for ARM/Thumb, <code>X0/X1</code> for AArch64, or <code>A0/A1</code> for RISC-V/MIPS. Run until the synthetic return boundary.</p><p>The expected return register is <code>R0</code>, <code>X0</code>, <code>A0</code>, or MIPS <code>V0</code>. The result is 5, except the MIPS examples deliberately add one in the delay slot and return 6. Compare assembly, trace, and Ghidra’s reconstruction. The exercise checks that selected observation, not full architecture expertise.</p>
<h3>Downloads and offline use</h3><dl><dt>Download lesson sources</dt><dd>Includes source, fixture descriptions, exact-byte/hash context, and build notes so you can inspect how the examples were made.</dd><dt>Download complete offline release</dt><dd>Fetches and hash-checks the release parts, then assembles the archive locally. Unzip it, keep the whole folder, run <code>python3 scripts/serve.py --port 4173</code> from the extracted release folder, and open <code>http://127.0.0.1:4173/</code>. The live site requires no such setup.</dd><dt>App source + engines / Church Git</dt><dd>Links to the source distribution and Church repository. The larger DiE operating-system source archive is separate; ordinary scanning does not need that download.</dd></dl>
<h3>FAQ</h3><dl><dt>Will a lesson erase my open sample?</dt><dd>Loading an exercise replaces the active investigation. Save or export first.</dd><dt>Why didn’t the evidence check pass?</dt><dd>Read its specific hint and confirm the same exercise is still loaded. These are targeted checks, not general grading or a complete record of your work.</dd><dt>Do I need to read the book in order?</dt><dd>No. Start with a question and the matching chapter. The Start here and glossary chapters are there when an unfamiliar term slows you down.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>Try the same small workflow on a sample you are authorized to study. Keep an observation, a tentative explanation, and one next question in Notebook.</p>`},
{id:'notebook',title:'Notebook',view:'notebook',summary:'Notes, annotations, complete projects, local save/resume, and what exports contain.',body:`
<h3>Save the understanding, too</h3><p>Full projects and library revisions now retain imported signatures, explicit indirect targets, table hypotheses, structure layouts and engine-applied signatures. Restoring them recomputes static evidence without executing sample code. Older projects start with empty new collections.</p><p>A signature written under Code notes is still a hypothesis. Choose <strong>Use signature in Ghidra</strong> to copy it into the engine controls, then decompile to validate and save it. Names follow their addresses across views. Native reconstructed code is marked out of date after corrections and must be rebuilt; SPECTRA does not quietly rewrite old C text.</p>
<h3>Keep the understanding, not just the binary</h3><p>A useful investigation records what you saw, how you got there, what you think it means, and what remains unknown. Notebook brings together string bookmarks/notes, case notes, code names/comments, and full workspace persistence. There are several save mechanisms; they preserve different amounts of work.</p>
<h3>A named library, with revisions</h3><ol><li>Give the investigation a name. Add a collection name to group related binaries, such as a client and its libraries or two firmware versions.</li><li>Choose <strong>Save new investigation</strong>. Original sample bytes, recovered bytes and the normal project evidence are included.</li><li>Choose <strong>Save updates here</strong> on a card, then use <strong>Save revision to selected investigation</strong> after more work. The sample hash must match; a different binary needs a separate investigation.</li><li>Use the card’s revision selector to open an earlier version or export it as a portable project. Opening replaces the active workspace and never automatically executes a target.</li><li>Search by name, collection, filename or hash. Move an investigation to Trash to hide it; use Show Trash to restore it. To free space, export anything needed, then use Delete permanently in Trash and confirm the deletion.</li></ol><p>The library keeps eight revisions per investigation; a ninth replaces the oldest. It holds up to 32 investigations and 512 MiB across retained revisions, including Trash. Browser quotas may be smaller and can change. The storage estimate covers the site’s storage, not just this library. Export anything you need to keep permanently. Clearing site data removes local saves.</p>
<h3>Case notes and saved evidence</h3><p><strong>Case notes</strong> is a free-text investigation log. <strong>Saved evidence</strong> contains starred strings; click one to return to it. Lightweight string notes and bookmarks save locally by sample hash when browser storage is available. Watch the save status instead of assuming storage succeeded.</p><p>A good short entry is: “Observation: function VA X refers to string at offset Y. Hypothesis: update request builder. Evidence: call and argument setup at Z. Missing: server behavior and runtime caller. Next: inspect the serializer.” Include the sample hash once near the top and keep original values separate from your interpretation.</p>
<h3>Code notes &amp; names</h3><ol><li>Enter a <strong>Code VA</strong> and choose <strong>Load note</strong>, or arrive through Name / annotate in Disassembly.</li><li>Set a name, optional address bookmark, and comment.</li><li>Record a <strong>Function signature hypothesis</strong> and <strong>Variable / type notes</strong> when useful. These are your notes, not automatic changes to the decompiler.</li><li>Choose <strong>Save code note</strong>. Names follow their address in supported code views. Save a full project to retain this wider annotation set.</li></ol>
<h3>Three ways to keep your work</h3><dl><dt>Lightweight local notes</dt><dd>Convenient per-sample string bookmarks and notes. They are not a full emulator/decompiler/recovery save.</dd><dt>Save full workspace on device</dt><dd>Stores the current complete workspace, including original sample/recovered bytes, in this browser’s IndexedDB. <strong>Resume saved workspace</strong> restores the latest saved workspace. This older control keeps one latest workspace slot. The Investigation library above it keeps separately named investigations and revisions.</dd><dt>Save project / Investigation project</dt><dd>Downloads a versioned ZIP for keeping, moving, or sharing the investigation. Use <strong>Restore project</strong> to load it. This is the portable backup option.</dd></dl>
<h3>Before exporting a full project</h3><p>Pause the debugger and let running analysis finish. The bundle can preserve annotations, settings, recovery bytes and provenance, DiE evidence, reconstructed source, dependencies, harness choices, comparison input, and replay checkpoints. Set <strong>Include original sample bytes in downloaded projects</strong> according to what you intend to keep/share.</p><p>Omitting the original sample does <em>not</em> make the ZIP metadata-only. Recovered bytes, supplied dependencies, the second comparison binary, captured state, and sensitive notes can still be present. A project without the main sample requires the matching local sample hash when restored. Check what you are sharing.</p>
<h3>Auto-save, restore, and forgetting</h3><p><strong>Auto-save the full workspace on this device</strong> is opt-in and includes bytes. It waits/debounces around changes and may need work to finish or the debugger to pause. Check the status if saving is unavailable. Browser quotas, private browsing, cleared site data, or eviction can remove local storage; export work that matters.</p><p>Restoring a project verifies bundled byte identities and can rerun static analysis to rebuild views. It does not automatically execute sample instructions. Debugger experiments arrive as evidence until you explicitly restore by replay. Managed browsing and comparison may take time again.</p><p><strong>Forget saved workspace</strong> deletes the latest full workspace slot and disables its autosave. It does not delete exported files, clear every lightweight per-sample note, or sanitize the machine. Use the browser’s site-data controls if you deliberately want to clear all local site storage.</p>
<h3>FAQ / saving problems</h3><dl><dt>Why is Save refusing to run?</dt><dd>A worker may still be running, the debugger may need a pause, no sample hash may be available, or storage/bundle limits may have been reached. Read the message; cancel/finish work or export a smaller investigation deliberately.</dd><dt>Why won’t a project restore onto this file?</dt><dd>The bytes may not match the recorded SHA-256. Reopen the exact sample rather than relabeling the project to fit another binary.</dd><dt>Does JSON report mean full project?</dt><dd>No. The top-level JSON/CSV/text findings exports focus on metadata and strings. Choose <strong>Investigation project</strong> for the full workspace.</dd><dt>Do saved notes teach Ghidra my types?</dt><dd>No. Apply actual supported type/signature assumptions in Decompile’s dedicated controls. Keep your reasoning here.</dd><dt>Is an imported checkpoint already verified?</dt><dd>It is imported evidence. Replay verifies its state against the same sample/engine before adopting a running emulator state.</dd></dl>
<h3>Where to go next</h3><p>End a session with a reproducible trail: exact artifact, addresses, observations, assumptions, experiment inputs, and one next step. Export the project, then return tomorrow without having to rediscover everything.</p>`},
{id:'workflows',title:'Workflows & troubleshooting',view:null,summary:'Routes through the workbench for clients, firmware, honeypots, patches, and dead ends.',body:`
<h3>Choose a route based on the question</h3><dl><dt>“What is this honeypot artifact?”</dt><dd>Record the hash → File structure → Detect → Strings → references for one meaningful value → Decompile the relevant function → Notebook. Keep “found in a honeypot” as collection context, not proof of every possible malicious behavior.</dd><dt>“How does this desktop client form its requests?”</dt><dd>Detect/structure → ILSpy if managed → type/method search and Network/Serialization clues → check auth/setup dependencies → select a small harness target → export a recipe for your authorized lab. The browser can explain and generate; a real client/server experiment still needs its environment.</dd><dt>“Where does this firmware handle a message?”</dt><dd>Firmware files → child provenance → structure/CPU/endianness → Strings/log messages → references → native Decompile → a small integer-routine experiment if the code does not require unmodeled hardware.</dd><dt>“Did this patch add the intended check?”</dt><dd>Two version hashes → Binary diff → confirm candidate function alignment → compare the changed branch and operation → trace callers and input origin → record what was and was not verified.</dd><dt>“Where does this hidden string come from?”</dt><dd>Look for a source blob and code reference → Recovery for a known encoding/key or stack-write pattern → Debugger for a small supported decoding routine → capture memory → save the transform/state provenance.</dd></dl>
<h3>When the tool says no</h3><p>Separate five common causes: wrong settings, missing environment, unsupported feature, bounded coverage, and malformed input. They lead to different next steps. A wrong CPU needs a corrected decoder; an unmodeled peripheral needs a different environment; a time cap needs a narrower question. None is solved by treating partial output as complete.</p><dl><dt>No results anywhere</dt><dd>Confirm the actual file and size. Check the basic-analysis warning, clear filters, then use Hex. A file need not contain executable code or printable strings.</dd><dt>Assembly is plausible but pseudocode is bizarre</dt><dd>Recheck the entry point, mapping, calling convention, code/data distinction, and analyst-supplied types. Look at callers and return paths.</dd><dt>Emulation stops on an unsupported API/instruction</dt><dd>Record that boundary. You have learned where this model stops. Do not claim the real application crashes or exits there.</dd><dt>An engine will not load</dt><dd>Check the visible load error and browser/network conditions. Hosted assets may be challenged/rate-limited; wait for the stated retry interval. Offline use needs the complete release and its local server, not one saved HTML page.</dd><dt>Large input makes the page slow</dt><dd>Cancel heavy work, narrow the range/function, close unneeded engine sessions, and export between focused investigations. A desktop-class modern browser is the practical target; low-end/mobile performance is not comprehensively validated.</dd></dl>
<h3>A note another person can reproduce</h3><p>Use this compact template in Notebook:</p><pre>Question:
Sample name + SHA-256:
Tool / engine / relevant settings:
File offset or VA (say which):
Observed bytes, code, or state:
Assumptions and supplied inputs:
Interpretation:
Alternative explanation / missing evidence:
Next check:</pre><p>That record serves beginners and professionals alike. A careful “not established yet” is more useful than a confident label with no trail.</p>`},
{id:'glossary',title:'Glossary & limits',view:null,summary:'The terms used throughout SPECTRA and the boundaries worth remembering.',body:`
<h3>Words you will keep seeing</h3><dl><dt>Static analysis</dt><dd>Inspecting stored bytes/metadata/code without executing the target’s instructions.</dd><dt>Dynamic analysis / emulation</dt><dd>Observing execution with specific inputs. Emulation supplies a modeled CPU/environment; it is not automatically equivalent to a real operating system.</dd><dt>Binary / image / assembly</dt><dd>“Binary” is a file of bytes. “Image” often means its loaded representation. “Assembly” can mean machine-instruction text, or a .NET metadata/code unit; use context.</dd><dt>Offset / VA / RVA</dt><dd>File position / mapped virtual address / address relative to an image base. Keep their labels attached to numbers.</dd><dt>Endianness</dt><dd>The byte order used to represent multi-byte values. Little-endian stores the least significant byte first; big-endian stores it last.</dd><dt>ABI / calling convention</dt><dd>Rules for arguments, return values, register preservation, data layout, and other interfaces between compiled components.</dd><dt>Entry point / function / basic block</dt><dd>A starting address / a recovered routine / a straight-through group of instructions ending at a flow boundary. These describe different scales of code.</dd><dt>Register / stack / heap</dt><dd>CPU state slots / memory commonly used for calls and local work / dynamically allocated memory. The browser model only supplies mapped state described by its setup.</dd><dt>Import / export / symbol</dt><dd>A declared external dependency / an exposed definition / a name associated with code or data. A stripped program has fewer retained symbols.</dd><dt>Cross-reference / call graph</dt><dd>A discovered link between locations / relationships among callers and callees. Static discovery can miss indirect relationships.</dd><dt>Indirect call</dt><dd>A call whose target comes from a register or memory value rather than a directly encoded destination.</dd><dt>Decompiler / disassembler</dt><dd>A tool reconstructing higher-level logic / a tool translating machine bytes into instructions for a CPU. Both depend on correct interpretation.</dd><dt>IL / CLR / managed metadata</dt><dd>.NET intermediate language / its runtime environment / structured type, method, and assembly identity information. Managed decompilation is different from native CPU emulation.</dd><dt>Encoding / compression / encryption / packing</dt><dd>Changing representation / reducing data size / protecting data with cryptographic parameters / wrapping program contents for later reconstruction. Similar-looking bytes can arise from very different processes.</dd><dt>Entropy</dt><dd>A statistic describing byte-value distribution, commonly displayed from 0 to 8 bits per byte here. It does not name an encryption algorithm or determine maliciousness.</dd><dt>Heuristic / signature / confidence</dt><dd>A clue based on patterns / a known identifying pattern / the tool’s evidence category or estimate. Always read the underlying evidence and limits.</dd><dt>Breakpoint / watchpoint</dt><dd>A stop tied to an execution location / a stop triggered by a watched memory access. In SPECTRA, watch hits pause after the access.</dd><dt>Harness / fixture</dt><dd>A driver for a controlled test / a known artifact used to verify expected behavior.</dd><dt>Provenance / hash</dt><dd>The recorded origin and transformation history / a digest identifying exact bytes. A hash establishes identity, not safety.</dd><dt>IOC</dt><dd>An indicator used in an investigation or detection workflow. A string that resembles a domain is not automatically a validated indicator of compromise.</dd></dl>
<h3>Practical limits in this edition</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Boundary to plan around</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>General sample</td><td>64 MiB; basic analysis has a 30-second worker deadline.</td></tr><tr><td>Disassembly</td><td>60,000 instructions / 4 MiB decoded bytes; incomplete coverage is reported.</td></tr><tr><td>Native decompile</td><td>32 MiB input; one selected function at a time and a bounded worker.</td></tr><tr><td>Managed decompile</td><td>16 MiB main assembly; up to 32 dependencies, each 16 MiB and 64 MiB total.</td></tr><tr><td>DiE</td><td>16 MiB sample; about 20 MiB first-use runtime; 180-second job limit.</td></tr><tr><td>Debugger</td><td>16 MiB guest mappings; 100,000 attempted operations/session; up to 10,000 instructions or four seconds/run; eight checkpoints.</td></tr><tr><td>Recovery</td><td>4 MiB transform input, 8 MiB output; eight layers / 16 MiB total; 4,000 rows.</td></tr><tr><td>Comparison</td><td>32 MiB per file; bounded function recovery and displayed results.</td></tr><tr><td>Firmware</td><td>64 MiB expanded container; 16 MiB regular member; 2,000 entries; supported archive formats only.</td></tr><tr><td>Investigation library</td><td>32 investigations, eight retained revisions each, 512 MiB total; browser quota may be smaller.</td></tr><tr><td>Projects</td><td>128 MiB bundle budget; browser storage may have less available space.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>These are limits of this release, not general laws of reverse engineering. Read the actual warning/status when a job stops; individual paths enforce additional bounds. Save your investigation before narrowing, clearing, or replacing evidence.</p>
<h3>Keep the learning loop short</h3><p>Observe → form a small hypothesis → choose a check → record the result → update the hypothesis. You do not need certainty about the whole binary to learn something real from one function.</p>`}
];
